course: dictionary
lesson: 6565
title: Nicknames and what they tell us
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A nickname is a short or friendly name used instead of someone's real name, such as «Liz» for Elizabeth. It can also be a name given because of something about a person, place or thing, like «the Big Apple» for New York. Nickname can also be a verb: to nickname someone means to give them one.
explain: Context helps you spot a nickname. «Friendly nicknames are fine when both people enjoy them.» The clue is the word friendly, and the sentence says both people must enjoy it. A nickname is only good when the person it is for is happy with it.
explain: Some nicknames are not kind. If someone uses a name you do not like to hurt you, that is teasing, and it is never okay. Always call people by the names they are happy with.
explain: Scientists use nicknames too. «Scientists think the Moon formed when a Mars-sized body, nicknamed Theia, smashed into the young Earth.» Theia is a nickname, not the official name, and the sentence says this is an idea, not something proven beyond doubt.
fact: A nickname is a name used instead of someone's real name, often a short or friendly one.
fact: «Nicknamed» means given a nickname, as in «nicknamed Theia».
fact: Places can have nicknames too, such as «the Big Apple» for New York.
fact: A nickname that hurts someone is teasing, so use names people are happy with.
fact: Nickname is a noun and a verb: you can have a nickname, and you can nickname someone.
exercise: «Friendly nicknames are fine when both people enjoy them.» What makes a nickname fine? | It comes from a book | It is the longest name you know | *Both people enjoy it | It is very short
exercise: Liz is a nickname for which name? | Lydia | *Elizabeth | Lucy | Lizard
exercise: «The Big Apple» is a nickname for which place? | *New York | London | Paris | Sydney
test: Your friend says a nickname hurts her feelings. What should you do? | Make up a new one for her today | *Stop using it and call her by the name she likes | Keep using it because it is funny | Use it louder in class
test: What does nickname mean as a verb? | To do sums | *To give someone a nickname | To forget a name | To run very fast
exam: Why might a nickname not be fine? | *It hurts someone who does not enjoy it | Nobody has used it before | It has too many letters | It is too short to say
